  "prompt_text": "The figure is a schematic overview of several transport and surface phenomena associated with topological materials. Across most panels, a flat sample is shown with applied current, magnetic field, magnetization, or temperature gradient, and a measured voltage, illustrating how different external conditions generate unusual electrical responses.\n\nThe first group highlights Hall-like effects: in the quantum Hall effect, a magnetic field and current produce a transverse voltage; in the anomalous Hall effect, magnetization plays the role usually associated with field; and in the anomalous Nernst effect, a temperature gradient together with magnetization generates a transverse signal. Related anomaly-driven transport is also shown through the chiral anomaly and a gravitational-anomaly-like thermal response, both involving parallel current or heat flow with magnetic field and a resulting voltage.\n\nOther panels emphasize nonequilibrium and surface-enabled behavior. The photogalvanic effect depicts incident light inducing a photocurrent without obvious electrodes driving it. Topological catalysis is represented by molecules such as H2O, H2, and O2 interacting with a surface, suggesting catalytic reactions promoted by the material. Finally, STM and ARPES are shown as probes of a crystal, indicating experimental methods used to study topological surface or electronic structure.",
